---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Screenshot from "../components/Screenshot.astro";
import UiLabel from "../components/UiLabel.astro";

// Issue #1105: "Family sharing and roles." The four role names render
// through UiLabel (#1106's build-time ARB component), so they always match
// the app: renaming a role in lib/l10n/app_en.arb fails this site's build
// instead of leaving a stale page. Both invitation directions get equal
// weight. Every claim carries a `cite:` comment and a site/claims.md
// entry (checked by test/site/site_claims_test.dart).

const description =
  "Share a cycle profile with the people who help look after it: four " +
  "guardian roles, single-use invitations in both directions, and alerts " +
  "that never carry what was logged.";
---

<BaseLayout title="Family sharing and roles" description={description}>
  <article class="doc">
    <h1 class="doc__title">Family sharing and roles</h1>
    <p class="doc__lede">
      A cycle affects more than one person sometimes. lunarlog is built for
      that from the ground up: profiles are shared with their guardians,
      every entry is attributed to the person who logged it, and the server
      — not just the app — enforces what each role may do.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>The four roles</h2>
      <!-- cite: role names from lib/l10n/app_en.arb (guardianRoleLabel*) via UiLabel; capability matrix from lib/domain/models/profile_guardian.dart (canLog, canEditProfile, canManageGuardians, canDeleteProfile); server-side enforcement per PRIVACY.md §5 "Her Own Profile" ("enforced by the server, not just the app"). -->
      <p>
        Each guardian on a profile holds one of four roles, and the role is
        the whole permission model:
      </p>
      <table class="role-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Role</th>
            <th scope="col">Can log entries</th>
            <th scope="col">Can edit the profile and manage guardians</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><UiLabel key="guardianRoleLabelPrimaryGuardian" /></th>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td>Yes — and only this role can delete the profile or transfer ownership</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><UiLabel key="guardianRoleLabelCoParent" /></th>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><UiLabel key="guardianRoleLabelCaregiver" /></th>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td>No</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><UiLabel key="guardianRoleLabelViewer" /></th>
            <td>No — view-only</td>
            <td>No</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §5 "Guardian Roles & Read Visibility" (read visibility is role-independent; the private-note exception). -->
      <p>
        What a role never changes is reading: every accepted guardian reads
        everything logged on the profile, whatever their role. The one
        exception is a day note the profile's subject marked private, which
        only they can read.
      </p>
      <figure class="screenshot-figure">
        <Screenshot
          screen="guardians"
          device="iphone-67"
          theme="light"
          alt="The Manage guardians screen for a shared profile, listing its guardians and their roles."
        />
        <figcaption>Managing the guardians of a shared profile.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Two ways to start, equally real</h2>
      <!-- cite: docs/product/positioning.md "Either side can start" ("Neither direction is the 'real' one"); PRIVACY.md §5. -->
      <p>
        Either side can start. Neither direction is the "real" one, and
        nothing in the app assumes a parent is the account holder.
      </p>
      <div class="direction-grid">
        <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §5 "Guardian Roles & Read Visibility"; single-use invite links in lib/domain/sharing/invite_links.dart (issue #129). -->
        <div class="direction-card">
          <h3>You track, and invite someone in</h3>
          <p>
            Your profile is yours first. Send a single-use invitation link
            to a parent, partner, or caregiver and pick their role — a
            read-only viewer, or a hands-on caregiver or co-parent. You can
            change the role or revoke it later.
          </p>
        </div>
        <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §5 "Her Own Profile (subject membership)"; issue #802. Under-13: §5 "Minimum-Age Policy"; issue #957. -->
        <div class="direction-card">
          <h3>You set up, and invite them in</h3>
          <p>
            Create a profile for a family member, then send the subject
            invitation — labelled "<UiLabel key="manageGuardiansPendingSubjectLabel" />"
            on the profile's guardian screen. When they accept, the profile
            shows up on their device as theirs —
            "<UiLabel key="profilePickerSubjectSubtitle" />", not a
            shared-with-you row — their own logging attributed to their
            name, while guardians keep full access in return. For someone
            under 13, this invitation is the only way the app begins, and
            accepting it is the parental-consent record.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §5 "Minimum-Age Policy" (13+ to create an account alone; under-13 only through the invitation). -->
      <p>
        One age rule runs under both directions: an account is for people
        13 and older, and someone younger uses the app only through the
        profile a parent created and invited them to.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Ownership can move with the family</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §5 "Two-stage custodian model"; lib/domain/sharing/ownership_transfer_service.dart (issue #4). -->
      <p>
        A profile a parent created starts under the parent's account. When
        the person it is about is ready to hold it themselves, the parent
        transfers ownership to their account in one step — every entry keeps
        its original "logged by" attribution, and the parent chooses whether
        to stay on as a co-manager or step back to read-only. After the
        transfer, the parent's access is an ordinary membership the new
        owner can revoke like anyone else's.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Merging, not overwriting</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §2.A "Same-date merge disclosures" (the losing value is kept briefly in a disclosure record; never in a notification; 30-day deletion). -->
      <p>
        If two guardians log the same date without knowing, lunarlog merges
        both entries and shows what happened: the value that lost the merge
        — say, one guardian's note — is kept briefly so its author can see
        and recover it in the app. That disclosure is visible only to the
        profile's guardians, never appears in a notification, and is
        permanently deleted after 30 days.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Reminders that reach the right person</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §4 FCM row (opt-in per guardian; push token + profile id only; "Never a note, tag, flow level, date, or profile name"); the fixed copy is lib/domain/notifications/scheduling.dart's kReminderTitle/kReminderBody, matched character-for-character by supabase/functions/_shared/notification_copy.ts. -->
      <p>
        A guardian can turn on optional push alerts — when another guardian
        logs an entry, or when an expected entry hasn't arrived — so the
        people around a profile stay in the loop without asking. The alert
        that reaches the lock screen is always the same fixed generic text,
        never the content: no note, tag, flow level, date, or profile name
        ever rides along. Alerts are opt-in per guardian, and a build with
        no push configuration never contacts a push service at all.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Sharing an estimate without sharing the log</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §5 "Prediction-Only Sharing Excludes Minors" (single-use code; estimate calendar only; never entries, notes, or guardian access; server-refused for minor profiles). -->
      <p>
        For adult profiles, the primary guardian can create a
        prediction-only connection: a single-use code that lets one other
        account see the estimated period and fertile days on a read-only
        calendar — never a logged entry, note, or symptom, and never
        guardian access. It is not available for a minor's profile, and the
        server refuses it there, not just the app.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="doc__footer-note">
      What guardians can and cannot read, and how the whole household's data
      is protected: <a href="/privacy-security">privacy and security</a>,
      and the full <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
